WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.879 --> 00:00:09.500
If you asked most of your friends which was
faster.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.530
The fastest production car in the world or
the fastest helicopter, I think most of them

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:14.980
would guess the helicopter.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:19.300
Intuitively we expect anything flying in the
air to have a higher top speed than anything

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:23.519
on the ground could achieve, but physics is
a cruel mistress and conventional helicopters

00:00:23.519 --> 00:00:29.089
are doomed to a max speed of just 400 km/h,
with that record being set over 30 years ago

00:00:29.089 --> 00:00:34.550
by John Trevor Egginton in a Westland Lynx,
while just this year the Koenigsegg Agera

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:40.360
RS demolished that record driving at 445 km/h,
and sure you don’t have to worry about traffic

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:45.050
in your helicopter or those pesky speed suggestions
on the road, but the bragging rights for top

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:48.060
speed will always go to cars for the rest
of time.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:53.160
So how can this be, what quirks of physics
are limiting helicopters from flying faster?

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.760
First let’s look at what limits a cars top
speed.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.870
To determine top speed, we first need to identify
the forces attempting to slow the vehicle

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:00.870
down.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:05.280
In space, with no resistance, even a small
force can continually accelerate an object

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.330
until it reaches close to the speed of light,
if it is maintained for long enough, which

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:11.659
would require a silly amount of energy.

00:01:11.659 --> 00:01:15.810
On earth though, every time we provide energy
to our vehicle, resistance in the form of

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:20.170
air resistance and rolling resistance in the
wheels are sapping it away.

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:24.090
Eventually we get to a point where the energy
we are providing the vehicle equals the energy

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:27.259
being taken away, and the vehicle cannot travel
any faster.

00:01:27.259 --> 00:01:32.439
In the case of cars the top speed is predominantly
determined by air resistance, for now we will

00:01:32.439 --> 00:01:35.920
ignore rolling resistance as it’s negligible
in comparison.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.009
The equation for drag force is given by this
equation, and the equation for power is simply

00:01:40.009 --> 00:01:41.619
force times velocity.

00:01:41.619 --> 00:01:44.640
Rearranging these variables we get an equation
for top speed.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:48.499
Applying this to the Agera RS specs, we find
it’s top speed almost perfectly with a decent

00:01:48.499 --> 00:01:52.530
degree of accuracy, considering we ignored
rolling resistance.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:56.740
Decreasing our drag coefficient and frontal
area also increases top speed, but to increase

00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:00.959
power we need to increase airflow to cool
the engine, so this is a difficult balancing

00:02:00.959 --> 00:02:01.959
act.

00:02:01.959 --> 00:02:05.609
We also discovered in a previous video that
designing rubber tires capable of withstanding

00:02:05.609 --> 00:02:10.759
these rotational speeds is an incredibly difficult
task, with the Bloodhound SSC opting for aluminium

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:12.500
wheels to break the land speed record.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:18.010
These are the limiting factors for a car,
so what are the limiting factors for a helicopter.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:21.780
Helicopters have to deal with all the same
problems as cars in counteracting aerodynamic

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:26.460
drag, but first a helicopter needs to overcome
the force of gravity, using the same rotor

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.700
that will need to provide forward thrust.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:31.340
So is this just an issue of needing engine
power?

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:32.370
Partially yes,

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:37.410
The Westland Lynx was powered by not one but
two Rolls-Royce Gem turboshaft engines, each

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:41.430
equaling the power of a single Agera RS twin-turbo
V8 engine.

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:45.580
Yet, even with twice the power, it still can’t
beat it in a straight line race.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:46.580
How can this be?

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:50.820
Let’s go on a journey as the helicopter
takes off and transitions to forward flight,

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:54.780
and see why it can’t go any faster even
with stronger material for blades or more

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:55.940
powerful engines.

00:02:55.940 --> 00:03:00.340
As the Lynx powers up the blades begin to
rotate faster, providing more lift according

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:01.340
to this equation.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:05.450
Where A is the swept area of the blades, Cl
is the coefficient of lift of the blades and

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:07.510
v is the velocity of the blades.

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:10.890
The coefficient of lift depends on a lot of
things, like the geometry of the blades and

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:15.220
angle of attack, but for the sake of simplicity
we will assume it is constant.

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:18.540
Once the lift is greater than the weight of
the helicopter it begins to rise, and when

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:21.980
it equals the weight the helicopter will enter
a hover state.

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:26.020
Now to go forward, the pilot will need to
transition some of the lift to thrust by angling

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:27.770
the rotor disk forward.

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:29.830
But here we meet our first problem.

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:33.350
Looking downwards, we can see that left-side
of our blade is moving backwards relative

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:37.100
to the the direction of travel, and our right
hand side to moving forward relative to the

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:38.320
direction of travel.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:42.600
Similar to planes, the aerodynamic surface
of the blade will generate more or less lift

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.490
depending how quickly it is moving through
the air.

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:48.170
So our right side generates more lift that
our left.

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:51.501
To counteract this the rotor integrates an
ingenious little mechanism, where the blade

00:03:51.501 --> 00:03:54.330
can change its angle of attack as it rotates.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:57.950
Here the advancing blade will have a lower
its angle of attack, thereby lowering it’s

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:01.880
lift, and the retreating blade will increase
it’s angle of attack, increasing it’s

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:02.880
lift.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:06.990
This helps equalise the lift across the rotor
disk, but this solution has it’s limit.

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:10.681
As we increase an aerofoils angle of attack
the lift increases, but eventually he hit

00:04:10.681 --> 00:04:15.290
a point of where flow separation occurs and
the aerofoil begins to generate less lift.

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:16.709
This is our first speed limit.

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:20.630
The helicopter will eventually hit a speed
where it cannot adjust the angle attack any

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:23.310
further to compensate to dissymmetry of lift.

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:25.840
However there are solutions to this problem.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:29.660
If we have two blades rotating in opposite
directions, we no longer have to compensate

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:33.540
for this dissymmetry in lift, as the two blades
will have the opposite dissymmetry of lift

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:35.380
and cancel each other out.

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:39.000
This is what allows the Chinook to cruise
along faster than any other military helicopter

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.560
at 315 km/h.

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:45.560
Now we have overcome one speed limit, but
even now the Agera RS is still speeding ahead

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:47.990
.
The next speed limit we reach is the sound

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:52.070
barrier, if we continue to increase forward
velocity, the tips of the advancing blade

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:54.210
will eventually break the sound barrier.

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:57.720
And while planes can handle breaking through
the sound barrier with the correct design,

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:01.009
they don’t need to pass through it several
hundreds of times a minute.

00:05:01.009 --> 00:05:05.050
This adds to the problems of dissymmetry of
lift, but also causes problems with varying

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:09.190
stress that fatigue the material of the blades
and ultimately lead to failure.

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:12.850
This speed limit poses a more difficult challenge
to overcome in our current configuration.

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:17.720
To travel faster, we need to increase thrust,
to increase thrust we need to increase lift.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:21.320
Let’s take a look at the equation from before
to see how we can do that.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:25.250
We can either increase our rotor speed, or
we can increase our blade diameter.

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:28.660
Increasing the blade velocity will obviously
make us more likely to break the sound barrier,

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:32.550
but so will increasing our blade diameter,
as the velocity of the blade increases as

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:34.360
we travel down the blade.

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:38.060
Designers have hit an optimum balance between
these two variable already, so that’s not

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:39.919
an option for increasing speed.

00:05:39.919 --> 00:05:42.370
So how else can we increase helicopter speed?

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:47.139
By converting more of that vertical lift to
horizontal thrust, but this poses a new problem,

00:05:47.139 --> 00:05:50.730
at some point were are going to hit a point
where the helicopter is not generating enough

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:52.830
lift to keep itself aloft.

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:56.040
The solutions to this problem are hitting
a point where the we can scarcely call the

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:57.889
aircraft a helicopter.

00:05:57.889 --> 00:06:02.840
Enter the Eurocopter X cubed, which holds
the unofficial helicopter speed record, unofficial

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:07.240
exactly because it is not strictly a helicopter,
as it generates a large portion of it’s

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:09.710
forward thrust from vertical propellers.

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:13.370
This decreases the burden of the horizontal
rotor to generate forward thrust allowing

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:17.760
it slow its rotational speed, minimising the
impact of our previous speed limits, the rotor

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:22.050
can also decrease it’s rotational velocity
as the helicopter gains speed as more lift

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:25.740
is generated from two small wings on either
side of the helicopter.

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:31.430
These design choices allowed the x cubed to
reach top speed of 472 km/h, beating the previous

00:06:31.430 --> 00:06:36.820
record held by the Sikorsky X2, and demolishing
anything ever achieved by a production car.

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:41.010
Pushing this design ideology even further
we completely blur the lines of what a helicopter

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:43.710
is, with tiltrotors, like the Osprey.

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:49.389
This aircraft has a top speed of 565 km/h,
this combined with it’s incredible vertical

00:06:49.389 --> 00:06:53.590
take-off capabilities, that are not hindered
by limited weight issues like jet engine powered

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:58.650
aircraft like the Harrier, has made it an
incredibly versatile tool for the US military.

